{"title":"Isometry","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>An <b>isometry</b> is a map which preserves <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Distance\" title=\"Distance\">distances</a> between <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">points</a>.  Isometries exist in any space in which a distance function is defined, i.e. an arbitrary abstract <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Metric_space\" title=\"Metric space\">metric space</a>.  In the particular case where we take our space to be the usual Euclidean plane or Euclidean 3-space (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> with the standard <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclidean_metric\" title=\"Euclidean metric\">Euclidean metric</a>), the isometries are known as <i>rigid motions</i> and two sets which can be transformed onto each other by an isometry are said to be <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Congruent_(geometry)\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Congruent (geometry)\"> congruent</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Isometries_are_injective\">Isometries are injective</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Isometries are injective\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since for any metric we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0faea11c11306cf47c9849d20c1f9ba127f1240c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(x, y) &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" /> whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/d/74ddb8309acb92903e40f1ba96475de8f7761cdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\neq y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" />, it follows that every isometry must be an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Injection\" title=\"Injection\">injection</a>.  \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Suppose otherwise.  Then there is some non-injective isometry <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/c/d0c65c4cf8fdaea432dfd49daf33e0ee482c998d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi: S \\to T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is not injective, we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/1/9d19885760a57808a0f193b25d08e3c2653523f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\exists x, y \\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/d/74ddb8309acb92903e40f1ba96475de8f7761cdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\neq y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/6/106905241b46079373c62d29816942f7d6fb7549.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(x) = \\phi(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"96\" height=\"20\" />.  But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/5/5f5808b0bf564e0edda9a8eef0b87a3f1e3bbcc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\neq y \\Longrightarrow d_S(x, y) &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"177\" height=\"18\" /> while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/0732746db706cd394d312fa7255418b298ca218b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(x) = \\phi(y) \\Longrightarrow d_T(\\phi(x), \\phi(y)) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"278\" height=\"18\" />, and this contradicts the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is an isometry.\n</p><p><br />\nNote that this does <i>not</i> mean that isometries are necessarily <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bijection\" title=\"Bijection\">bijections</a>.  Consider, for example, the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Discrete_metric\" title=\"Discrete metric\">discrete metric</a> on the integers, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/f/7bf1b1cdf6094eee69a950e1c52d4499c36be855.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\mathbb{Z}, d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/9/5c99a80be71f31d291cf82b421f9e8257485d30e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(x, x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/4/0d49e3913a8fc15741bead34e890132e00e1e73d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\neq y \\Longrightarrow d(x, y) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"167\" height=\"18\" />.  It is simple to verify that this is a metric space.  The map <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3ac6b65e3de5504aebf96511a74e3410f5ca54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi: \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" /> given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/a/69a47aa68d2b950f8914b4157e2519ff2cdf63df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n) = 2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> is an isometry but it is not <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surjection\" title=\"Surjection\"> surjective</a>.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Isometry&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018074113\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.018 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.032 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 77/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 265/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3151/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.520      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    6.520      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3940:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018074113 and revision id 10164. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}